  m_Name: MushroomCatalog
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ForagAR.Library.MushroomCatalog
  m_Entries:
  - displayName: Puffball Mushroom
    prefab: {fileID: 8829377244055881403, guid: 83da7cfce345c3342859735ace0b118e, type: 3}
    thumbnailOverride: {fileID: 0}
    scientificName: 
    description: "Puffball is a common name for a group of mushrooms belonging to several genera, including <i>Lycoperdon</i>, <i>Calvatia</i>, and <i>Bovista</i>. Unlike typical mushrooms, puffballs do not have visible caps with gills or stems. Instead, their fruiting bodies are usually round, pear-shaped, or oval, with a smooth, warty, or spiny outer surface.\n\nTheir size varies considerably depending on the species. Small puffballs may measure approximately 1–5 cm in diameter, while larger species, such as the giant puffball (<i>Calvatia gigantea</i>), can reach 20–50 cm or more across. Young puffballs are generally white or pale cream, with firm, solid flesh inside. As they mature, the interior gradually becomes yellowish, olive-brown, and eventually dark brown as the spores develop.\n\nThe outer skin, known as the peridium, surrounds the spore-producing tissue. When mature, the skin of many species develops an opening or breaks apart, allowing the spores to escape as a cloud of fine, powdery dust when the mushroom is squeezed, struck, or disturbed by raindrops. This characteristic spore release is the origin of the name \"puffball.\"\n\nA key identifying feature is the interior of a young puffball: it should be uniformly white and firm, without any developing mushroom structure. This is particularly important because young specimens of some poisonous <i>Amanita</i> mushrooms can resemble puffballs before their caps emerge."
    habitat: "Puffballs are saprotrophic fungi, meaning they obtain nutrients by breaking down dead and decaying organic matter. Different species grow in different environments: many are terrestrial and occur in grasslands, meadows, lawns, gardens, forest clearings, and along woodland paths, while others grow directly on decaying logs, tree stumps, and other dead wood.\n\nThey are widely distributed across temperate regions of the world, including Europe and North America. They commonly appear from summer through autumn, although the exact fruiting season depends on the species and local climate. Some species grow individually, while others form dense clusters or groups. Giant puffballs are particularly associated with open grassy areas, fields, and meadows."
    uses: "Edible in some species, but only when young and correctly identified. Many common puffballs, including the common puffball (<i>Lycoperdon perlatum</i>) and giant puffball (<i>Calvatia gigantea</i>), are considered edible when their interior is completely white, firm, and uniform. They have a mild, earthy mushroom flavour and can be sliced and cooked, for example by frying or sautéing.\n\nAs puffballs mature, their internal flesh turns yellowish, olive-brown, and eventually into a powdery mass of spores. At this stage, they are unsuitable for eating and may cause digestive upset. The mushroom must be cut vertically from top to bottom before consumption to check its interior."
    lookAlikes: "Correct identification is essential: some young, egg-shaped poisonous mushrooms, including deadly <i>Amanita</i> species, can resemble puffballs externally. A genuine puffball should have a completely uniform white interior without any developing cap, gills, or stem. Earthballs (<i>Scleroderma</i> species), which can also resemble puffballs, are poisonous or potentially toxic and often have darker internal flesh. <b>Never eat a wild mushroom based on its external appearance alone.</b>"
